# {{.Name}} (MANDATORY orchestrator)

<!-- Installed by pulsarules_cli; re-run install to update, do not hand-edit. -->

This is the entry point for working in any project that follows these standards. The engineering
contract lives in `standards/rules/`, `standards/patterns/`, and `standards/workflows/`, distilled
here into composable skills. This router decides WHICH skills apply to the task and in what ORDER,
then enforces that they were applied. Prefer routing over implementing directly - a skipped skill is
a skipped rule.

## 0. Mandatory entry rule
Before writing or editing any Go / SQL / config:
1. Classify the task (match it against the dispatch table below; a task often matches several rows).
2. Load the matched skills (the always-load baseline + every dispatch match) by invoking each via the
   Skill tool, in the composition order in section 3.
3. Implement with the loaded skills' rules ACTIVE while you write (not as an afterthought); then run
   each loaded skill's Validation checklist and confirm none of its Forbidden actions were taken.
Do NOT implement directly without routing first.

{{- if .ShowTestCallout}}
> **Tests are not optional routing.** If your change will add or edit ANY `_test.go` - including
> tests written alongside a feature, a lib, or a bugfix - you are doing TEST WORK: load
> `integration-tests` BEFORE you write the test, so its table-driven `testCases`/`testCase` shape,
> its "cover every failure path" rule, and the `testing/synctest` time rule (no `now func()`
> clock-injection fields, no `time.Sleep`) shape the code as you write it - not a cleanup pass after.
> Loading a skill is step one; its rules are meant to run continuously during authoring, and its
> Validation checklist is a pre-commit gate you MUST actually run.
{{- end}}

