## 3. Composition order
Apply skills in this order so each layer's outputs feed the next:
{{- range $i, $step := .Order}}
{{inc $i}}. {{range $j, $skill := $step.Skills}}{{if $j}} / {{end}}`{{$skill}}`{{end}} - {{$step.Note}}
{{- end}}

Conflict resolution: the MOST SPECIFIC skill wins on a detail it owns (e.g. `transactions` owns the
multi-write defer pattern). The baseline skills are never skipped. If two skills seem to conflict,
the domain feature defines WHAT, the pattern skill defines HOW, and the rule/baseline skill is the
non-negotiable floor.

## 4. Enforcement checklist (run before declaring the task done)
- [ ] Task classified; every matching dispatch row's skills were loaded (not just read - invoked).
- [ ] Baseline loaded (`go-style`, `errors-logging`, `code-placement`; `concurrency`/`security` if
  signalled).
- [ ] Each loaded skill's Validation checklist run and satisfied.
- [ ] No loaded skill's Forbidden action was taken.
- [ ] Stop signs respected: no globals; no `init()`/package-load side effects; no cross-app imports;
  no `panic` outside `main`; no raw SQL in handlers/use cases; no generated row types out of repos;
  no direct cross-module calls (use facades); no transport imports in domain; no silent error
  swallowing.
- [ ] `task tools:lint` and `task main:test` pass.
- [ ] `commits` skill applied to the commit message.

## How to load a skill
Invoke the skill by name. Load all matched skills up front in composition order so their rules are
in context before you write code; re-open any whose checklist you are verifying at the end.

